WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.850
Lattice enthalpy is the energy needed
to turn one mole of an ionic solid,

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.310
such as salt, into separate gaseous ions.

3
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.390
No one can grab a salt
crystal and pull it apart,

4
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.090
ion by ion, to measure that energy.

5
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:20.250
Yet the data booklet gives a value for
sodium chloride: 790 kilojoules per mole.

6
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.870
So how do we know the lattice
enthalpy of sodium chloride?

7
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.110
In this mission, you'll meet the steps
that turn elements into gaseous ions:

8
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.590
atomization, ionization
and electron affinity.

9
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.050
You'll read Born–Haber cycles for
sodium chloride and calcium chloride.

10
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:42.450
Your mission: explain how chemists know a
lattice enthalpy that no one can measure.
